# Scoped to .github/hooks/ in the target project. Ships WITH the hooks, because the hooks are
# useless without it.
#
# The baseline's own root .gitattributes is in `alwaysExcluded` and is never copied into a target,
# so before this file existed a project inherited 15 bash hooks and none of the line-ending
# protection that keeps them runnable. `core.autocrlf=true` is the Windows default, so git rewrites
# these to CRLF on checkout, the shebang becomes "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r", and the kernel looks for an
# interpreter literally named "bash\r". Every bash hook then dies with
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory".
#
# It does not bite on Windows, where the .ps1 variants run — it bites the colleague who clones on
# macOS or Linux, or CI, which is the worst possible place to discover it.
#
# This file deliberately scopes only to hooks/. It sets no policy for the project's own source.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell hooks are ASCII with a UTF-8 BOM. Windows PowerShell 5.1 — which the Copilot host uses —
# reads BOM-less files as cp1252, and the mojibake breaks string terminators. Git leaves the BOM
# alone, but pin the endings so a clone matches the bytes that were actually tested.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
